FWIW — some implementation details from infra that were helpful on prior integrations, and a small sample of a rate-limit-safe HTTP wrapper you can drop into your worker pool.

1) Connection pooling: ensure each worker uses a shared HTTP client (Node: keepAlive) to avoid socket churn.
2) Header surface: preserve X-Request-ID from your systems so Redwood can map logs to your traces.
3) Backoff on 429: prefer retry-after header if present; otherwise use client jittered backoff.

Small Node wrapper (illustrative):
async function safeCall(payload) {
  let attempt = 0;
  while (attempt < 5) {
    try {
      const res = await client.generate(payload);
      if (res.status === 200) return res;
      if (res.status === 429 || res.status >= 500) throw new Error('transient');
    } catch (e) {
      attempt += 1;
      const delay = Math.random() * Math.min(5000, 200 * Math.pow(2, attempt));
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, delay));
    }
  }
  throw new Error('max retries exceeded');
}

Endpoint migration note: for the dual-write stage, include a header X-Redwood-Shadow: true so we can toggle logging and suppress billing on the shadow requests if desired. We'll coordinate to disable billing for shadow runs during testing.

